---
import { readFileSync } from "node:fs";
import { parse } from "yaml";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { loadSponsors } from "../lib/sponsors";
import { latestDay } from "../lib/data";

const { weights, top_n, category_floor, max_per_category } = parse(readFileSync("../config/scoring.yaml", "utf8"));
const day = latestDay("computational");
const { contact } = loadSponsors();
---

<Base description="How TrendBio tracks and ranks computational biology research, tools and industry activity." canonicalPath="/about/" title="About — TrendBio" lastRun={day?.run.finished}>
  <article class="prose" aria-labelledby="about-heading">
    <h1 id="about-heading">About TrendBio</h1>
    <p>TrendBio tracks what is moving in computational biology and bioinformatics. Each day, it groups related papers into research topics and attaches closely related code and industry evidence and ranks the Top {top_n}, with evidence and a synthesis for readers who already know the field.</p>
    <h2>Sources</h2>
    <p>We collect preprints from bioRxiv and arXiv, published papers from PubMed, code and tool activity from GitHub, and announcements from industry feeds. Each topic links to its underlying evidence, with preprints linked to their published versions when known.</p>
    <h2>How ranking works</h2>
    <p>Topics are ranked using four weighted terms: rise ({weights.rise * 100}%), diversity ({weights.diversity * 100}%), code velocity ({weights.code_velocity * 100}%), and volume ({weights.volume * 100}%). Rise compares recent paper activity with a topic’s historical baseline, or a typical cluster when history is limited; diversity rewards a mix of evidence types, code velocity measures GitHub star gains, and volume reflects paper count. A small penalty for topics that ranked yesterday encourages rotation.</p>
    <p>Code and industry items attach to at most one research topic when their similarity clears a calibrated threshold. They contribute evidence, diversity and code velocity; paper counts, rise and topic continuity stay paper-only. The separate “From industry” list shows up to eight relevant announcements first seen in the preceding seven days, whether or not they attach to a topic.</p>
    <p>Code appears in the “From code” list because repositories rarely join research topics by text similarity.</p>
    <p>“Shared by researchers” covers Bluesky only and never affects rankings or scores. It excludes accounts linking 20 or more papers a week and can include authors promoting their own work.</p>
    <p>Selection reserves {category_floor} topic per represented category, then fills by score with at most {max_per_category} per category. The selected topics appear in score order; quieter days may have fewer than {top_n} topics.</p>
    <p>Weekly (ISO Monday–Sunday) and calendar-month rankings sum each topic’s stored daily scores, break ties by days ranked then unique items, reserve one place per represented category and cap each at five within the Top 20, and show days ranked, best rank, unique items and maximum rise for the period; incomplete periods are labelled “so far”.</p>
    <h2>Machine-generated summaries</h2>
    <p>Headlines, summaries and syntheses are machine-generated and may contain errors. Follow the evidence links and consult the original sources before relying on a claim.</p>
    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p><!--email_off--><a href={`mailto:${contact}`}>{contact}</a><!--/email_off--></p>
  </article>
</Base>
